WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.152 --> 00:00:02.610
SPEAKER 1: It should
be no surprise

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.970
that using any cloud
resource tends to cost money.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.900
When it comes to managing
your Google Cloud costs,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.730
you'll want to be able to
understand what your costs are,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.460
put controls in place
so you don't overspend,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.930
and then optimize
your costs from there.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.530
Let's see how you can understand
your Google Cloud costs

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.670
and optimize virtual machines
running on Compute Engine.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.260
The best way to
understand your costs

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.680
is to use the billing reports
available in the cloud console.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.170
Just use the left
navigation to click Billing

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.100
and choose your billing account.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.580
Here's a quick
summary of your costs.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.440
If you want to drill
into further detail,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.990
choose Reports on the left side.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.440
Here, you'll be able to see your
costs broken down by project

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.350
so you can quickly tell where
your costs are coming from.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.870
In addition, you can
group costs by product

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.910
to see which Google Cloud
services are making up

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.110
your costs.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.320
And then filter by SKU to
see a more detailed breakdown

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.010
of your usage.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.530
So that's how you
can get a better

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.820
understanding of your costs.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.450
Now let's look at some
ways to optimize your costs

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.099
for Compute Engine.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.290
Compute Engine automatically
applies discounts

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.230
for running a virtual machine
over a sustained period

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.260
of time.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.350
These are called
sustained use discounts

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.320
and don't require any
configuration to enable.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.110
You can save up to 30% on
a virtual machine running

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.100
during a significant portion
of the billing month.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.850
If you know that you'll
be using resources

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.970
over a long period of time,
you can save even more

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.420
by using committed
use discounts.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.170
These are great for
static workloads where

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.230
you have consistent resource
usage, like multiple production

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.400
machines.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.430
To see what your usage looks
like, head to the Commitments

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.470
tab on the left navigation.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.080
Here, you can see how many
compute resources you're

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.438
using, like VCU cores,
RAM, local SSD, or GPUs.

00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:50.730
If there's a certain amount
you know that you'll always

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.040
be running, such
as a baseline, you

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.800
may want to sign up for
commitments for a one or three

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.460
year term, which can
save you up to 57%.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.668
Here, for example, you can see
that this graph is pretty flat.

00:02:02.668 --> 00:02:04.710
So there's not a lot of
fluctuation in the amount

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.750
of resources being consumed.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.210
This would make for a great
candidate for purchasing

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.220
commitments to save money.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.700
In addition, commitments can
be made per project or span

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.672
across an entire
billing account.

00:02:16.672 --> 00:02:18.630
So you can set up the
scope that makes the most

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.525
sense for your organization.

00:02:20.525 --> 00:02:21.900
If you want to
ensure that you're

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.060
maximizing the
commitments you have

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.745
and the potential cost
savings that you could have,

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:28.830
the committed use
discount analysis report

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.780
is the best place to start.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.970
If you actually want to
purchase a commitment,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.640
head to the compute page and
click on Purchase Commitment

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.200
to get started.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.330
Coming soon are
automatic recommendations

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.620
for your committed use discounts
based on your historical usage.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.020
Applying these recommendations
can provide an easy way

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.970
to save on long term usage.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.820
But if you have spiky
or dynamic workloads,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.740
there are other ways
to save on your costs

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.300
that you should consider.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.370
To start, Google
Cloud automatically

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.770
provides recommendations
for machines and disks

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.800
that may be idle
or underutilized.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.240
In the recommendation
column, you

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.660
can see suggestions for each VM
based on its historic resource

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.020
usage, such as adding or
removing resources or even

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.760
deleting them entirely.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.580
Thanks to the ability to resize
your VMs to custom machine

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.830
sizes, you can fine
tune your workloads

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.190
to be exactly the right size
and not pay for any resources

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.610
that you're not using.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.610
If your machines are
already the right size,

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.920
but there are times when
you don't need them,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.070
you can also stop the
instances to save money.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.040
When a VM is stopped, you
don't pay for the Cores or RAM

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:32.970
running.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.370
For example, you may be
running a test environment

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.690
that you don't need outside
of core business hours.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.610
So it can be shut off.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.120
You can even use some quick
Cloud Functions code and Cloud

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.420
Scheduler to turn your VMs
on and off automatically

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.428
when you do or don't need them.

00:03:47.428 --> 00:03:49.470
Depending on the type of
workload you're running,

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.650
you may also benefit from using
different types of instances

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.990
and pre-emptable VMs.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.570
The newer generation of E2
general purpose machines

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.210
can give you similar
performance to the N1 series

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.100
while also saving you money.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.530
In addition, memory
optimized and compute

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.860
optimized instances may be
better fits for your machines,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.670
letting you focus in on exactly
those resources that you need.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.230
Leveraging
pre-emptable instances

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.960
is a great way to run stateless
workloads that aren't time

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.540
sensitive, like genomics
or media transcoding.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.810
They have a maximum
uptime of up to 24 hours

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.620
before they're deleted,
but they're 80% cheaper.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.405
You can choose pre-emptable when
making a new compute instance,

00:04:28.405 --> 00:04:30.030
but you'll likely
want to automate this

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.720
by spinning up new pre-emptable
machines when you need them.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.080
Every workload is different.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.100
So it's important to understand
what your resource uses looks

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.490
like and which tools you can
use to optimize your costs based

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.110
on your unique needs.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.540
Start with the billing reports
to understand your costs.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.332
And then see which of
these recommendations

00:04:47.332 --> 00:04:48.660
works best for you.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.030
Check out this link to see
videos and documentation

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.650
about more ways to manage
and optimize your costs.

